/*  altShadowT -- THE F-15 REGRESSION CHECK. Written 2026-08-18 as a negative
    control that had to FAIL, graduated the same day when the fix landed.

    WHAT F-15 WAS. In parse, the arm that runs testAttributes was tested before
    the arm that runs testOptions, and the two are chained with or, so any rule
    owning an attribute never reached the alternation walk. testAttributes
    skips every noPrint attribute and returns false when they were all skipped,
    so an alternation rule that acquired one noPrint attribute matched nothing
    at all and the enclosing parse ran off the end of its input. That is what
    stopped the generated parse walk: it hangs a noPrint body on the rule.

    HOW IT WAS FIXED. Tony ruled the members arm goes first, gated on the rule
    not carrying data, so that container rules keep their old routing.

    ⚠ THIS FIXTURE'S VERDICT INVERTED WHEN THE FIX LANDED, DELIBERATELY. In its
    first form the pass condition was that row B could NOT be parsed and the
    sentinel could NOT be reached. Do not read the history as a bug in the
    fixture. Rule H6: a parked pin that starts passing graduates, and this is
    the graduated form.

    THE THREE ROWS.

    Row A hangs one noPrint attribute on a members-shaped rule. No body, no
    compile, nothing to do with code generation. This is the poison.

    Row B is an action call with an argument, and its shape is load-bearing. A
    first draft used a print line and it parsed cleanly even under the defect,
    which would have read as no defect present. The poisoned rule sits under
    the token rule, so it is reached when the parser resolves a name followed
    by an invocation argument. A bare keyword statement never asks for it.
    Match the probe to the path or the probe answers a different question.

    Row C is the VACUITY GUARD and it is what makes rows A and B mean
    something. A poison that never landed also lets row B parse, so row C
    walks the attributes of the poisoned rule and names what it finds. If the
    poison is absent, row C says so and the whole run asserts nothing.

    READING IT. All three rows plus the sentinel must appear. A missing
    sentinel means the run truncated, which is the defect returning.  */
Start();
include(unitTests);
include(utilities);
search reset stack Grokking UnitTests Utilities list;
register(AltShadow);
define
    dummy;
    rowB argument code={
        print "B ok    the invocation parsed -- the alternation survived the poison":;
        };
    rowC argument code={
        iterate asCur attributes on argument;
        while ++asCur;
            print "C guard poisoned rule carries attribute" ~taG:;
        };
    poison argument code={
        dud <- copyOf(dummy);
        dud :. noPrinT;
        argument +% dud;
        print "A ok    poisoned" ~taG "with one noPrint attribute":;
        };
    ;
poison(ANYorNum);
rowB(dummy);
rowC(ANYorNum);
print "ALTSHADOWT SENTINEL -- run reached the end":;
stop();
